<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_paragraph"><a href="#A2.SSx1.SSS0.Px1" title="In Detailed Model Reasoning (Contrastive Questions) ‣ Appendix B Appendix B: Case Study ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">Q1: What is a key feature of the object in the image?</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_paragraph"><a href="#A2.SSx1.SSS0.Px2" title="In Detailed Model Reasoning (Contrastive Questions) ‣ Appendix B Appendix B: Case Study ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">Q2: Which of the following is unique to the object shown?</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_paragraph"><a href="#A2.SSx1.SSS0.Px3" title="In Detailed Model Reasoning (Contrastive Questions) ‣ Appendix B Appendix B: Case Study ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">Q3: How does the object differ from others?</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_paragraph"><a href="#A2.SSx1.SSS0.Px4" title="In Detailed Model Reasoning (Contrastive Questions) ‣ Appendix B Appendix B: Case Study ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">Q4: What is visible in the image?</span></a></li>
</ol></li>
</ol></nav>
</nav>
<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div id="infobox" class="infobox">
  <a id="license-tr" href="https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available">
    License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license
  </a>
  <div id="watermark-tr">
arXiv:2509.15059v1 [cs.HC] 18 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tenghao Ji &amp; Eytan Adar
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Michigan
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Images play a vital role in improving the readability and comprehension of Wikipedia articles by serving as ‘illustrative aids.’ However, not all images are equally effective and not all Wikipedia editors are trained in their selection. We propose QuizRank, a novel method of image selection that leverages large language models (LLMs) and vision language models (VLMs) to rank images as learning interventions. Our approach transforms textual descriptions of the article’s subject into multiple-choice questions about important visual characteristics of the concept. We utilize these questions to quiz the VLM: the better an image can help answer questions, the higher it is ranked. To further improve discrimination between visually similar items, we introduce a <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Contrastive QuizRank</span> that leverages differences in the features of target (e.g., a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Western Bluebird</span>) and distractor concepts (e.g., <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mountain Bluebird</span>) to generate questions. We demonstrate the potential of VLMs as effective visual evaluators by showing a high congruence with human quiz-takers and an effective discriminative ranking of images.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:505.9pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Wikipedia guides editors to select “images [that] look <span id="Sx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">like</span> what they are meant to illustrate” (emphasis in original) and to serve as an “illustrative aid” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wikimedia, 2025</a>)</cite>. Images are important in improving the comprehension of textual content by a reader <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark and Lyons, 2010</a>)</cite> and numerous studies show that well-integrated visuals can improve comprehension, retention, and engagement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harp and Mayer, 1997</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carney and Levin, 2002</a>)</cite>. However, selecting these images is challenging. As of September 2025 there are over 7 million article pages in the English Wikipedia and 127 million images on the Wikipedia commons. Editors may not know what makes a good image or will need to make trade-offs in their choices (e.g., should we show a bird in context? in all its variations?). The result may be that, for many pages, images can be generic, unclear, misaligned, or poorly matched to the content of the article, thus limiting their communicative effectiveness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rama et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Navarrete and
Villaespesa, 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are natural opportunities to help editors by automatically identifying good images based on a concept’s textual description<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
          A <span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">concept</span> is often the subject of a Wikipedia article, but we allow for an article to talk about multiple <span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">concepts</span>, each may have their own image</span></span></span>. Existing efforts have focused mainly on similarity metrics between text and image captions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal et al., 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aletras and
Stevenson, 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leake et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh, Zouhar, and
Sachan, 2023</a>)</cite> or isolating representative examples from an image set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Other approaches have tried to directly use the LLM/VLM as a judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> by prompting the model to evaluate the image’s fit to a section (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang and Lin, 2024</a>)</cite>). However, these approaches can be somewhat coarse and misaligned from the actual intent of the image–to serve as an educational aid. To address this, we propose QuizRank, a system that directly uses learning assessments to rank possible images. Quizzes have two main functions. The first is to assess an individual student. The second, which is key for our approach, is that assessments serve as programmatic evaluations. That is, how effective was the teacher/module/lesson, or in our case <span id="Sx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the image</span>, in improving learning?</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15059v1/QuizRankAlg.png" id="Sx1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/274;" width="698" height="274" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Example of the QuizRank algorithm being applied with both standard (top) and contrastive ranking (bottom). In both situations a ‘quiz’ is generated based on the description of the target option (and, in the case of contrastive, one or more distractors). The VLM will attempt to answer the questions for each of these quizzes per image allowing us to rank them. Note: the example questions and images here are only illustrative, but a complete run and analysis for the Guija/Chandrakala case is available in the Appendix.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">QuizRank (graphically depicted in Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) analyzes the text of the article to identify the visual characteristics of the concept (e.g., “the statue is bronze”). From these, the system constructs a set of multiple choice questions (e.g., “what is the statue made from? A) Bronze, B) Marble, etc.”). By coupling the images and this generated exam. QuizRank uses a vision-language model (VLM) to score each possible image. The intuition is that the more helpful the image is for the VLM in taking the quiz, the better the image.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To select better images, QuizRank can generate quizzes on both the original concept and through an analysis of reasonable distractors (<span id="Sx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contrastive question generation</span>). For example, in Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we have two possible images for a target concept—Gujia, a kind of fried Indian pastry—and a third image for a Chandrakala (a different fried pasty). A good image would be a perfect ‘representation’ of the target concept, while at the same time highlighting the distinguishing features of the concept relative to similar concepts. Our contrastive setup—which draws inspiration from past work on visual disambiguation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leggett and Kirchoff, 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bruce and
Humphreys, 1994</a>)</cite>—encourages the model to focus on specific differences between similar entities and improves the robustness of image selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate QuizRank, we focus on the correspondence between the VLM’s performance with a human’s on answering questions given different images. We show that the VLM is roughly analogous to the human performance in both scoring images and distinguishing in-class vs. out-of-class images. We demonstrate the effectiveness of the approach in selecting images on a wide array of Wikipedia concepts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Related Work</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work builds on previous research on question generation, multimodal learning, visual comprehension in educational contexts, and Wikipedia-based image evaluation.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Question Generation</h5>

<div id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">QuizRank directly builds on work in question generation (QG). Original efforts in the domain used highly templatized and domain-specific approaches (e.g.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kurdi et al., 2020</a>)</cite>). However, with the prevalence of LLMs, more modern QG systems provide more sophisticated analysis of text for question generation (e.g.,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Richards Maldonado, Abouzied, and
Gleason, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shimmei, Bier, and
Matsuda, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moore et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu and Wang, 2024</a>)</cite>). QuizRank uses a similar prompting approach to construct questions, but focuses on visual properties. Additionally, we develop an approach for generating contrastive questions that can help distinguish both misclassified and out-of-class objects by emphasizing the visual properties of the concept.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Multiple choice questions (MCQs) consist of a question stem, a correct answer, and distractors. Although simple a the high level, these still need to be well designed to ensure proper assessment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rodriguez, 2005</a>)</cite>. In building QuizRank, we apply established guidelines and good practices (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haladyna, 2004</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Little et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schwartz, Tsang, and
Blair, 2016</a>)</cite>) to create our MCQs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Images in Wikipedia and Multilingual Visual Knowledge.</h5>

<div id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Psychological studies have demonstrated the positive effect of illustrations on comprehension and learning (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carney and Levin, 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Because of this, dictionaries and encyclopedias include illustrations and images for many specific purposes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stein, 1991</a>)</cite>. Similarly, there are many benefits to integrating images into a Wikipedia article <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wikimedia, 2025</a>)</cite>. However, past work on Wikipedia images highlighted the challenges of image selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rama et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. This reinforces the need for assessments for image appropriateness and educational value.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Silva et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silva et al., 2024</a>)</cite> constructed a benchmark of 94 Wikipedia concepts with 470 multiple-choice questions to test: general knowledge (e.g., <span id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">when was the tower built?</span>); visual feature recall (e.g., <span id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">what color is the Western Bluebird’s chest?</span>); and concept recognition (e.g., <span id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which of these images represents a Mountain Bluebird?</span>). Their study, which tested human participants, found that the utility of the image in Wikipedia is highly dependent on the semantic alignment between the image and the content of the article. We use the same benchmark, but shift focus to evaluating image relevance through vision-language models (VLMs) rather than human annotators.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-modal Representation and Vision-Language Alignment.</h5>

<div id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are a number of datasets that provide image-to-text alignment. For example, Srinivasan et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srinivasan et al., 2021</a>)</cite> introduced a multilingual Wikipedia-based image–text corpus that provides grounding for training and evaluating VLMs. Our work builds on efforts to link text to images, but focuses on learning objectives centered on the visual properties of the objects.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work has demonstrated that vision-language pretraining using natural language supervision enables transfer to many downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. We leverage this idea to design visual comprehension questions that can differentiate between semantically appropriate and ‘distractor’ images.
Other efforts categorized different types of image–text relations, offering taxonomies for alignment assessment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gyselinck and
Tardieu, 1999</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marsh and
White, 2003</a>)</cite>. In contrast, our method evaluates alignment functionally through QA performance. Finally, Wikimedia’s own Add-an-Image initiative <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wikimedia, 2022</a>)</cite> explores crowd-based image suggestion tasks. Our framework offers a scalable alternative using VLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these studies motivate our approach: to move beyond subjective human assessments by building an objective, interpretable framework that measures an image’s semantic and visual relevance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">QuizRank Design</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">QuizRank utilizes a multi-stage method to evaluate and rank image quality as illustrative aids. A central goal of the algorithm is high-quality illustrative images. We define high quality as those images that are good <span id="Sx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in-class</span> and <span id="Sx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">between-class</span> representatives.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In-Class Representation</span>: Good images will illustrate key visual features of the target concept. For example, if it is important that a statue is made of bronze, is 9 feet tall, and is missing an arm, the image should show this. To operationalize this feature, we focus our generated questions so that the VLM will validate if the image contains the feature. In other words, the better the image, the more help it will provide to the VLM in answering the questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Between-Class Representation</span>: Good images will also highlight the specific properties of the concept that <span id="Sx3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are distinct from related concepts</span>. For example, if a person were to learn what a Western Bluebird looked like from the image, would they later be able to distinguish a Western Bluebird they see in the wild from a Mountain Bluebird? We operationalize this by creating questions for the VLM to answer that when given in-class images, the VLM will be more likely to answer correctly than seeing distractor images (i.e., good discriminative questions).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">QuizRank achieves this through four steps (see Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). First, we generate MCQs from the article’s content (1a) and/or MCQs that also take into account distinctions with related articles–a contrastive refinement step (1b). For each image we are considering, we feed this quiz to a VLM (2) to see how many it can answer based on the image. Finally, based on how well each image performs to ‘help’ the VLM answer the questions, we can rank the images (3).</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Base Question Generation</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">QuizRank leverages past work on generating multiple choice questions using LLMs. The input to this process is the concept’s Wikipedia page. This can be optionally focused to those sub-sections that are more likely to contain visual properties of the concept (e.g., Architecture, Appearance, Description, Characteristics, Design, Details, etc.). The LLM prompt (see Appendix) was designed to utilize ‘good’ multiple choice design approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haladyna, 2004</a>)</cite>. QuizRank’s prompt uses chain-of-thought style prompting with an emphasis on targeting those features that might be inferred visually from an image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. To achieve this, it emphasizes that features such as texture, material, and structure should be prioritized. To reduce the likelihood that the question will ‘leak’ information about the concept itself, the prompt tries to enforce generalizations. For example, for Donatello’s statue of David<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
            <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">https://w.wiki/BGsp</span></span></span></span>, QuizRank generated the questions: “What material is the statue made of?” (with <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bronze</span>, <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Marble</span>, <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Iron</span>, and <span id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wood</span> as possible answers); “What is the male figure wearing?” (the article mentions the hat); and “What notable expression does the figure have?” This type of generalization reduces the ability of the model to make inferences (i.e., the LLM may be aware that the Donatello’s David is made of Bronze) and better simulates the information a human might use in answering the questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We do not enforce a minimum or maximum number of questions as this is often correlated with the number of visual features described in the article. Short articles provide fewer visual descriptions than long ones. In our data (see below) we find that QuizRank produces a minimum of 4 questions and a maximum of 11 (with a mode of 5). This number naturally depends on the length of the Wikipedia article’s characterization of the visual properties.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Contrastive refinement</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An encyclopedia is not intended as a field guide (e.g., to help birders identify and distinguish between birds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton, 2021</a>)</cite>). However, images as illustrative aids should not only serve as good exemplars of the target class, but should also emphasize the key distinctive qualities of that class in relation to others. For example, we can consider images of the Caiman<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
            <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">https://w.wiki/5Cvt</span></span></span></span>, a type of alligator that has interesting skin and teeth. We can look at an image and ask questions about its skin and teeth to see if that image is a good representative. However, because the Caiman shares properties with other related animals (e.g., the Chinese Alligator), looking at images of non-Caiman alligators may also generate the correct result. In situations where we want to find images that are good instructional aids in knowing what a Caiman looks like, these questions might not help.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To better support between-class effectiveness, QuizRank implements a contrastive refinement step (Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-1b). Specifically, we utilize a prompt that can take both the visual properties of the target concept and one or more similar concepts to first identify the distinct and overlapping features of these concepts (see the Appendix for the complete prompt). From the <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unique</span> visual characteristics, we are able to generate a more specific set of questions. Note that we do not <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">always</span> need to generate contrastive questions. In some situations there are simply no similar concepts, and the VLM is unlikely to answer correctly given out-of-class images. In our implementation, we test these out-of-class images (e.g., of the Chinese Alligator) on the base questions. Only in those situations where the VLM answers the class-specific questions (e.g., about the Caiman) <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correctly</span> with the out-of-class images do we generate contrastive questions. In Wikipedia, related contrastive concepts are selected from related linked articles or from other concepts/topics in the same Wikipedia class (e.g., <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Crocodilians by location</span>). Note that if we were to generate all possible questions about the visual properties of a concept, this approach may not be as valuable. However, creating contrastive questions may be a more cost-effective approach in that we can focus on only important questions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Quizzing the VLM</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To determine if an image is good, we can submit the image to the VLM with our MCQs to see if the image helps the VLM answer the questions (prompt in the Appendix). The more questions the VLM correctly answers, the more confident we can be that the image is good.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure that the VLM is using the image as evidence, we attempt to prevent the model from utilizing information other than what is in the image. As described before, we take steps to avoid leaking the concept’s identity by never explicitly mentioning it in the questions. In addition, the prompt strictly instructs the model to rely solely on visible content in the image, ignoring prior knowledge, indexed facts, or textual context. As a final check, the prompt also tries to force the VLM to generate reasoning that describes why it chose the answer given what it saw in the image. For example, in response to the question about the statue’s material, the model reasoned:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">To</span><span id="lstnumberx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">determine</span><span id="lstnumberx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">material</span><span id="lstnumberx1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">of</span><span id="lstnumberx1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">statue</span><span id="lstnumberx1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">based</span><span id="lstnumberx1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">on</span><span id="lstnumberx1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">image</span>,<span id="lstnumberx1.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">I</span>’<span id="lstnumberx1.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">ll</span><span id="lstnumberx1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.26" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">analyze</span><span id="lstnumberx1.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.28" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx1.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.30" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">visual</span><span id="lstnumberx1.31" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">characteristics</span>:
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx2" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx3" class="ltx_listingline">1.<span id="lstnumberx3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>**<span id="lstnumberx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Color</span><span id="lstnumberx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">and</span><span id="lstnumberx3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Texture</span>**:<span id="lstnumberx3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">The</span><span id="lstnumberx3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">statue</span><span id="lstnumberx3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">has</span><span id="lstnumberx3.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">a</span><span id="lstnumberx3.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">dark</span>,<span id="lstnumberx3.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">glossy</span><span id="lstnumberx3.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">appearance</span><span id="lstnumberx3.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">that</span><span id="lstnumberx3.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">resembles</span><span id="lstnumberx3.25" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.26" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">metal</span>.<span id="lstnumberx3.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.28" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">This</span><span id="lstnumberx3.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.30" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">sheen</span><span id="lstnumberx3.31" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">is</span><span id="lstnumberx3.33" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.34" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">typical</span><span id="lstnumberx3.35" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.36" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">of</span><span id="lstnumberx3.37" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.38" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">metals</span><span id="lstnumberx3.39" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.40" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">like</span><span id="lstnumberx3.41" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.42" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">bronze</span>.
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx4" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx5" class="ltx_listingline">2.<span id="lstnumberx5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>**<span id="lstnumberx5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Detailing</span>**:<span id="lstnumberx5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">The</span><span id="lstnumberx5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">fine</span><span id="lstnumberx5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">detailing</span><span id="lstnumberx5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">in</span><span id="lstnumberx5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx5.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">hair</span><span id="lstnumberx5.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">and</span><span id="lstnumberx5.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx5.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">facial</span><span id="lstnumberx5.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">features</span><span id="lstnumberx5.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">suggest</span><span id="lstnumberx5.25" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.26" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">a</span><span id="lstnumberx5.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.28" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">material</span><span id="lstnumberx5.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.30" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">that</span><span id="lstnumberx5.31" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">can</span><span id="lstnumberx5.33" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.34" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">hold</span><span id="lstnumberx5.35" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.36" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">intricate</span><span id="lstnumberx5.37" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.38" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">designs</span><span id="lstnumberx5.39" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.40" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">well</span>,<span id="lstnumberx5.41" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.42" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">like</span><span id="lstnumberx5.43" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.44" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">bronze</span><span id="lstnumberx5.45" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.46" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">or</span><span id="lstnumberx5.47" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.48" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">marble</span>.<span id="lstnumberx5.49" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.50" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">However</span>,<span id="lstnumberx5.51" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.52" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx5.53" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.54" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">color</span><span id="lstnumberx5.55" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.56" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">leans</span><span id="lstnumberx5.57" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.58" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">more</span><span id="lstnumberx5.59" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.60" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">towards</span><span id="lstnumberx5.61" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.62" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">bronze</span>.
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx6" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx7" class="ltx_listingline">3.<span id="lstnumberx7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>**<span id="lstnumberx7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Overall</span><span id="lstnumberx7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Visual</span>**:<span id="lstnumberx7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">The</span><span id="lstnumberx7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">statue</span><span id="lstnumberx7.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">doesn</span>’<span id="lstnumberx7.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">t</span><span id="lstnumberx7.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">show</span><span id="lstnumberx7.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx7.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">grain</span><span id="lstnumberx7.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">typical</span><span id="lstnumberx7.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">of</span><span id="lstnumberx7.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">wood</span>,<span id="lstnumberx7.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.25" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx7.26" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">light</span><span id="lstnumberx7.28" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">color</span>,<span id="lstnumberx7.30" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.31" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">and</span><span id="lstnumberx7.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.33" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">translucence</span><span id="lstnumberx7.34" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.35" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">of</span><span id="lstnumberx7.36" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.37" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">marble</span>,<span id="lstnumberx7.38" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.39" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">nor</span><span id="lstnumberx7.40" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.41" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">does</span><span id="lstnumberx7.42" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.43" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">it</span><span id="lstnumberx7.44" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.45" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">have</span><span id="lstnumberx7.46" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.47" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx7.48" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.49" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">heavier</span>,<span id="lstnumberx7.50" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.51" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">less</span><span id="lstnumberx7.52" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.53" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">refined</span><span id="lstnumberx7.54" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.55" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">appearance</span><span id="lstnumberx7.56" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.57" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">of</span><span id="lstnumberx7.58" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx7.59" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">iron</span>.
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx8" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx9" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Based</span><span id="lstnumberx9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">on</span><span id="lstnumberx9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">these</span><span id="lstnumberx9.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">observations</span>,<span id="lstnumberx9.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx9.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">most</span><span id="lstnumberx9.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">likely</span><span id="lstnumberx9.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">material</span><span id="lstnumberx9.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">is</span><span id="lstnumberx9.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">A</span>)<span id="lstnumberx9.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx9.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Bronze</span>.
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx10" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Correct</span><span id="lstnumberx10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Answer</span>:<span id="lstnumberx10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">A</span>)<span id="lstnumberx10.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx10.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Bronze</span>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The prompt also allows for the answer, “I can’t answer that based on the image.”</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Answer-based image ranking</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A final step (Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-4) calculates the overall quiz score to rank the images as a form of model-as-judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In the simplest instance, we can calculate the normalized number of correct answers for each image and rank based on this. That is, how many questions did each image help the VLM answer? However, there are situations where no <span id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">one</span> image can answer all the questions correctly. QuizRank can keep individual scores (e.g., a matrix of images versus questions) to identify <span id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bundles</span> of images that may be useful. Other types of scoring (e.g., clustering questions based on correlated answers, weighting by question difficulty, or the importance of different properties) may also be possible but are left for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Evaluation</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate our approach, we focus on two high-level objectives. First, we would like to validate the <span id="Sx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correspondence</span> of the VLM with human perception. Ideally, the VLM will answer the questions as a human would, using only information from the image. If the VLM benefits from the instructional aid (the image), we can infer that a human may also benefit.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span>: Do humans and VLMs answer questions in similar ways when observing the image?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span>: Does the VLM rely on external information (e.g., training text) when answering?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we validate the <span id="Sx4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">effectiveness</span> of the approach in isolating better images on Wikipedia.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3</span>: Can QuizRank identify better images than Wikipedia’s crowdsourced approach?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4</span>: How much variation is there in the quality of available in-class images on Wikipedia?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we end with a brief analysis of the stability of QuizRank results under different quiz sizes.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Data and Methods</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted two primary experiments to answer our research questions. We started with the 94 concepts selected in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silva et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (we refer the interested reader there for full details). Briefly, the concepts were chosen to be from popular, but less known Wikipedia pages. Concepts were selected from a wider range of Wikipedia categories: biology, architecture, food and drink, and art. From the original 94 concepts, we excluded five because they either focused on non-visual subjects (e.g., biographical articles) or were too short to generate meaningful visual questions. The Wikipedia articles for the 89 concepts were submitted to QuizRank for question generation, initially using the non-contrastive pipeline. We used OpenAI’s GPT-4o as an LLM model (with additional validation using LLaMA-4-Scout-17B) to generate the per-concept quizzes.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each concept, we curated four images: three images depicting the target concept (selected from the Wikipedia Commons) and one image of a visually similar but incorrect object (a distractor). The distractor was chosen by identifying concepts within the broad class (e.g., the Chinese Alligator for the Caiman). To avoid selecting obviously bad images, we required that an image be used on a Wikipedia article page (any language). Each image and the associated quizzes were submitted to GPT-4o for the VLM step of QuizRank (again validating with LLaMA). If the distractor image performed similarly to the correct images (i.e., difference in score <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> 2), we triggered the Contrastive QuizRank approach to create new quizzes and a new grade.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We collected human quiz scores on a subset of 40 concepts. We recruited 320 participants through the Prolific platform and administered the task via a Qualtrics survey (payment for this task was $1-$1.25). Each participant was randomly assigned five concepts. For each concept, participants were shown one randomly selected image for the concept and asked to answer the questions generated by QuizRank. We aimed for each image to be evaluated by two different participants. This required 320 participants (40 concepts <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 4 images <math id="Sx4.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 2 labels). As with the VLM experiments, our goal was to understand: did quiz performance improve with the presence of the image?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">VLM-Human Correspondence</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compared the average accuracy per image from both VLM and human sources across the 40 concepts (see Figure <a href="#Sx4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ VLM-Human Correspondence ‣ Evaluation ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Each point represents a single image: blue circles denote images illustrating the correct concept (Images 1–3), while red asterisks denote distractor images.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15059v1/figures/scatterplot1.png" id="Sx4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:279/279;" width="279" height="279" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Per-image average accuracy comparison between human participants and the VLM. Red points represent distractor images. The dotted line indicates parity.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We observe a strong correlation (Pearson <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.6125" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6125</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.6125</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>). This supports the idea that the images that were the most effective in answering the questions helped both humans and VLMs in the same way (<span id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span>). We also note that the distractors were generally located in the lower left quadrant, indicating that the baseline questions had some discriminative power. In general, the answers produced by the VLM tended to be higher than those of humans. This may be due to individual differences (e.g., cultural or concept familiarity/interest) and different levels of attention. It may also reflect a stronger inferential ability for the VLM (i.e., it can better reason through the choices). In practical use of QuizRank, this may not be a critical factor. Although the baseline score (the floor) may be higher, all images will be evaluated by the VLM. Thus, it is unlikely that any specific image will have some advantage.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Open-source models</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We re-tested the full QuizRank pipeline using the open-source LLaMA-4-Scout-17B model. The setup mirrored our primary experiment on 40 concepts (4 images per concept, with 3 in-class and 1 distractor image), allowing direct comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our results show strong alignment between LLaMA and both the human and GPT-4o judgments. The Pearson correlation between LLaMA’s image rankings and those of human participants was <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.613" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.613</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.613</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=7.51\times 10^{-18}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>7.51</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=7.51\times 10^{-18}</annotation></semantics></math>), nearly identical to what we observed with GPT-4o (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.6125" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6125</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.6125</annotation></semantics></math>). In terms of agreement with GPT-4o rankings, we computed the per-concept Spearman correlation and found that: 28 concepts had very strong correlations (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>); 4 concepts were strong (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.6</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.79</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.79</annotation></semantics></math>); 2 concepts were moderate (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.4</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.59</annotation></semantics></math>); 3 each were weak (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.2</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.39" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.39</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.39</annotation></semantics></math>) or very weak (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.19</annotation></semantics></math>). Overall, 32 out of 40 concepts (80%) showed strong or very strong agreement with GPT-4o, and 26 concepts (65%) had perfect rank correlation. In general, these findings reinforce the consistency and robustness of the QuizRank method across different models and the results are likely to improve with newer models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Between Concept Separation</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#Sx4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Between Concept Separation ‣ VLM-Human Correspondence ‣ Evaluation ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> provides an aggregate view of the score distributions for human and VLM responses across distractor and non-distractor images. Human participants performed significantly better on non-distractor images (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.44" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.44</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.44</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=0.18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=0.18</annotation></semantics></math>) than on distractors (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.26</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.26</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=0.14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=0.14</annotation></semantics></math>). This difference is statistically significant according to both Kruskal–Wallis (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=30.42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>30.42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=30.42</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>) and ANOVA (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=33.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>33.72</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=33.72</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>) tests. Similarly, the VLM also scored significantly higher on non-distractor images (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.66</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.66</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=0.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=0.23</annotation></semantics></math>) than on distractors (<math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.30</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=0.19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.19</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=0.19</annotation></semantics></math>), with both statistical tests confirming the distinction (Kruskal–Wallis: <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=53.33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>53.33</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=53.33</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>; ANOVA: <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=83.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>83.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=83.03</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When directly comparing human and VLM performance on non-distractor images, the VLM significantly outperformed humans (Kruskal–Wallis: <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=57.73" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>57.73</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=57.73</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>; ANOVA: <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=70.84" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>70.84</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=70.84</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>). On distractor images, the VLM still scored marginally higher, but the difference was smaller and not significant (Kruskal–Wallis: <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=0.75</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.3857" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3857</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.3857</annotation></semantics></math>; ANOVA: <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=1.155" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.155</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=1.155</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.286" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.286</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.286</annotation></semantics></math>). Anecdotally, the open-source model made a few more mistakes in producing a ranking that could separate the distractor, but these were limited.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="Sx4.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:212.5pt;"><img src="2509.15059v1/figures/boxplot.png" id="Sx4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:293/208;" width="293" height="208" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Score distributions for human and VLM across distractor and non-distractor images. Green triangles denote means.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="Sx4.T1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:242.8pt;">
<table id="Sx4.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx4.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison</span></th>
