</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">VLM Memorization</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key concern for any generic LLM or VLM is that certain knowledge will become ‘baked in, and the model will have memorized the answer based on the text or because it has learned the caption for a certain image. For example, the <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gayer-Anderson cat</span> is an Egyptian statue made of bronze with gold ornaments. This is a distinguishing characteristic of the stature that is captured in numerous text sources. If the VLM or LLM previously learned this information, the model may be able to correctly answer the questions regardless of the image content<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
              In fact, for this example, GPT-4o does know the answer</span></span></span>. As described above, we endeavored to ensure that the prompts to not ‘leak’ certain information to the model, and we force reasoning on the image rather than prompt/quiz text. In developing QuizRank, we did not identify cases in which the VLM used information outside of what the image provided in its reasoning. In reality, we are less concerned about hints that the VLM can infer from the <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">question</span> (i.e., if the quiz hints at an answer) as it will raise the scour floor for all images, but the ranks will likely remain the same.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, if the image has been indexed, the VLM may be able to identify it and answer questions based on that identification. As a quick validation that the model’s performance is not due to prior exposure to the test images (e.g., memorization or internal indexing), we conducted a controlled experiment. We used the eight concepts in our dataset that satisfied the constraint of having two images: one <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">old</span> image that was uploaded to Wikimedia Commons before August 1, 2019, and one <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">new</span> image uploaded after August 1, 2024. For this experiment we used the model <span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-2024-11-20</span>, which was trained on data up to October 2023. Thus, any image uploaded after August 2024 is unlikely to have been part of the model’s training set. In other words, images uploaded after the latter threshold were not seen during the training of the VLM (i.e., unindexed), whereas those before the former threshold were likely part of the model’s training data.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Both images were evaluated using the same set of questions generated from the article text. The results indicate no significant difference between old (likely indexed) and new (likely unindexed) images. Specifically, in 4 out of 8 concepts, both images led to the same number of correct answers; in 1 case, the indexed image outperformed the new one by one question; and in 3 cases, the unindexed image scored higher by one question. Image pairs were selected to convey comparable visual content and quality. This minimizes the risk that differences in question-answering performance are due to image clarity, composition, or informativeness, rather than indexing status (<span id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">In-Class Representation Quality</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By comparing to human performance, we have evidence that QuizRank can identify images that may be better for instructional aids. However, it is a question of whether this is actually needed in Wikipedia: does the underlying ‘wikiwork’ already lead to the selection of good images?</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To test this, we looked at the popularity of images (i.e., how many different pages an image is used in). If editors are effective, popularity might imply high quality. However, one language’s editor may copy images from another (usually English <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2018</a>)</cite>) which may lead less effective choices to stick around and become popular.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted a focused experiment on 13 random Wikipedia concepts (of the 89). For each concept, we selected 20 candidate images from Wikimedia Commons and recorded their frequency of usage—that is, the number of Wikipedia pages on which the image appears (for each of the 13 <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 20 = 260 images). We computed a popularity score for each image as <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{10}(\mathrm{usage\ count}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>usage</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>count</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{10}(\mathrm{usage\ count}+1)</annotation></semantics></math> to account for skew and zero values. Each image/quiz pair was independently evaluated by the VLM. For each image, we computed the percentage of correctly answered questions and then standardized these scores within each concept using z-scores. This normalization allowed comparison across concepts with differing question counts and difficulty levels.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15059v1/correlation_popularity_vlm.svg" id="Sx4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:279/200;" width="279" height="200"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Correlation between image popularity (log scale) and standardized model performance (z-score of percent correct) across 13 Wikipedia concepts.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#Sx4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ In-Class Representation Quality ‣ Evaluation ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows a plot of image popularity (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-axis) against standardized model performance (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-axis). We observe a very weak positive trend, suggesting a weak correlation between an image’s popularity and QuizRank score. However, there are clearly images that, for the purpose of serving as an instructional aid, may be better than the ones that are used (<span id="Sx4.SSx3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3</span>). This is not to say that there are not good reasons for selecting ‘sub-optimal’ images. This may be for aesthetic reasons (a better composed or less blurry picture), or because the editor may be optimizing for some subset of visual properties. However, we believe that presenting the QuizRank rankings to an editor may be an opportunity to help them select better images.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Contrastive Pipeline Evaluation</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#Sx4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ VLM-Human Correspondence ‣ Evaluation ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> partially demonstrates the ability of QuizRank to distinguish in-class images from distractors. We further analyze this based on the use of the contrastive question mechanism. For each of the 89 concepts we used one in-class image (the one used for the concept on the English language page) and an out-of-class distractor. Across the 89 concepts, we first evaluated model responses using the original basic (i.e., non-contrastive) question sets. In 57 concepts, the correct image outperformed the distractor image based on the VLM answers. In 26 concepts, the original and distractor images achieved equal scores. In only 6 cases did the distractor image outperform the in-class image. However, in 51 concepts, the difference in the number of correctly answered questions between in-class and distractor was 2 or less.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For concepts requiring further disambiguation (the 51 concepts), we applied the contrastive question generation mechanism. Among the 51 concepts that qualified, the original image performed better in 34 cases, performed equally in 16 cases, and was outperformed in only one case. These results suggest that our question-based image evaluation framework is generally robust and that the contrastive prompting strategy successfully improves discrimination when objects are visually similar.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Within-Concept Variance</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One thing we would like to know is that the questions we generate actually have variance in score (we do not want all in-class images to score the same). As shown in Figure <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Within-Concept Variance ‣ Evaluation ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the distribution of per-concept variances reveals that both humans and the VLM assign different scores to images within the same concept. This suggests that the generated visual questions are sufficiently discriminative for distractor removal (e.g., misclassified or bad images) but also fine-grained ranking among plausible images.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15059v1/figures/boxplot2.png" id="Sx4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:558/369;" width="558" height="369" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Box plot of per-concept score variance across non-distractor images. Both Human and VLM show non-zero median variance, indicating discriminability among visually relevant candidates.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This finding aligns with prior work on visual image-text alignment in Wikipedia, which emphasizes that subtle visual differences can significantly affect comprehension and user perception <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silva et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srinivasan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Our results indicate that vision-language models, when guided by well-designed prompts, can reflect these distinctions through answer-based evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Quiz Size</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is difficult to know exactly how many questions are optimal for a quiz. This number may depend on the key visual characteristics described in the article (recall that shorter articles will yield fewer questions). Additionally, while we discouraged the LLM from generating similar questions the reality is that providing questions that are small variants of each other can better assess true understanding over random guessing. The ‘optimal’ number of questions is hard to know and likely varies depending on the concept.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, we were curious if the rank stability of images changed significantly based on the number of questions. To test this, we started with 10 questions per concept (which we anecdotally found to offer coverage of visual concepts over most articles in our test test). We randomly removed questions from this to produce quizzes of length 9, 8, and so on. We then calculated the Spearman rank correlation between the image lists produced by QuizRank for each concept relative to the 10 question quiz. Figure <a href="#Sx4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Quiz Size ‣ Evaluation ‣ QuizRank: Picking Images by Quizzing VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows these results. While we do not advocate for any specific number of questions, around a 6-question quiz we are just shy of a <math id="Sx4.SSx6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.9</annotation></semantics></math> correlation and any larger test exceeds that.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15059v1/rank_stability.png" id="Sx4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:335/201;" width="335" height="201" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Rank stability of concepts relative to a 10-question quiz.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Discussion</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">QuizRank presents a novel approach to selecting the most visually appropriate image for Wikipedia articles using VLMs. Rather than relying on captions or prior indexing, our method evaluates images purely on their ability to help answer content-based visual questions. This acts as a proxy for the usefulness of the image, as a representative example of that concept. Contrastive question generation mechanism further enables disambiguation between visually similar candidates, an important step beyond surface-level matching. Our results provide evidence of the viability of the approach. However, it is worth considering how such a system would be implemented in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The problem of selecting images for Wikipedia articles shares characteristics with other editorial processes (e.g., picking images for textbooks, guidebooks, encyclopedias or dictionaries). However, there are also a number of unique constraints. Wikipedia images have specific intellectual property features (they need to be free) and it is likely that neither the editors or image creators (e.g., photographers or illustrators) are trained. Additionally, not all topics have the same number of images, making some selection tasks easy and others much harder. For example, we see a range from the <span id="Sx5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Nainamo bar’s</span> (a dessert common in Nainamo, Canada) 15 images, to the <span id="Sx5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Eastern Bluebird’s</span> over 800, and the Indian rice beer <span id="Sx5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hariya</span> with none. This is due to in part to the unequal availability of images due to the concept’s popularity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and how contextually constrained it is (can someone take a picture of it?).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There are a number of opportunities to use QuizRank in other Wikipedia tools for editors. For example, rather than ranking images, QuizRank could also highlight gaps in existing images and encourage the collection or creation of new illustrative aids. Other extensions might take into account how questions could be weighted (e.g., through editor control or by looking at ‘longer-lived’ Wikipedia edits about the way the concept looks). Future work may also include aesthetic features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shahid et al., 2014</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings echo prior educational psychology research suggesting that well-chosen visuals can significantly enhance comprehension and engagement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carney and Levin, 2002</a>)</cite>. In future work, we aim to extend our approach along several dimensions. First, we plan to generalize the framework to multilingual Wikipedia editions, which will require adapting both question generation and answer evaluation to respect language-specific descriptions, naming conventions, and cultural visual references. Second, we seek to enhance model robustness when dealing with culturally nuanced or historically sensitive concepts, where subtle visual cues or symbolic imagery may require careful treatment. To this end, incorporating human-in-the-loop validation and region-specific training data may help improve trustworthiness and fairness. Finally, we envision the development of interactive authoring tools that allow Wikipedia editors to collaborate with VLMs during image selection, offering question-based justifications, visual comparisons, and transparency into model decision-making, thus making AI-assisted curation both accessible and accountable.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx5.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Limitations</h3>

<div id="Sx5.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although our approach demonstrates promising performance in selecting visually relevant images for Wikipedia articles, several limitations remain. First, our framework assumes that article content can be effectively translated into multiple-choice questions. This excludes concepts that are inherently abstract, historical, or biographical, where visual representation is unavailable or difficult to evaluate visually. Questions that focus on more than simple visual properties may be useful in expanding our approach. Second, our indexing control experiments provide some evidence of visual grounding, but stronger guarantees would require model transparency or architectural constraints. Third, the quality of generated questions is influenced by prompt engineering and concept length; very short or poorly written articles may yield sparse or low-quality visual questions. Finally, although we selected a variety of topic areas, our evaluation is limited to curated concepts in English Wikipedia. Future work should assess generalizability across languages, domains, and larger-scale deployments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Conclusions</h2>

<div id="Sx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce QuizRank, a system to rank images for use as illustrative aids. The central feature of our approach is that illustrative aids should be evaluated as educational interventions. By generating assessments directly from the content of a Wikipedia article, QuizRank can leverage a VLM to determine the effectiveness of the image in helping answer the questions. QuizRank offers additional features to generate contrastive questions that not only isolate good images for the class, but can highlight unique features of that class. Our evaluation determines the ability of VLMs to perform as a human would when viewing images and questions, and the effectiveness of QuizRank in identifying good images.</p>
</div>
</section>
